{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A panel of trained non-experts following an explicit checklist is more reliable than a single expert for verifiable tasks because it replaces the expert's 'stochastic intuition' with a 'deterministic audit trail' that eliminates omission errors.","counterconditions":["The task requires synthesis of information that cannot be decomposed into a checklist.","The checklist is so comprehensive that it induces 'compliance fatigue,' leading to superficial checking (SCR_008).","The 'trained non-experts' lack the baseline literacy to interpret checklist prompts accurately."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The checklist acts as a cognitive forcing function that externalizes the expert's internal heuristic. While the expert may skip 'obvious' steps (omission error), the panel is structurally prevented from doing so. Reliability gain is achieved by shifting the failure mode from omission (invisible) to commission (visible/traceable), which is more easily corrected in verifiable outcomes.","optional_refs":["f9d456a6572a171c270f70cec40c3dfafd6472b14300a5ca2ea4e17d70bfd358"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["f9d456a6572a171c270f70cec40c3dfafd6472b14300a5ca2ea4e17d70bfd358","ba195d863b44e05fae4e064eb581457ba6a5501527a698d6ca85c6dc2ebe731a"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The single domain expert remains more reliable because the 'reliability' of a checklist panel is an illusion of precision that fails catastrophically when the case falls outside the checklist's predefined scope.","counterconditions":["The task is highly standardized with very few edge cases.","The checklist is dynamically updated based on a comprehensive database of previous failures."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expertise is not a set of steps but a mental model of the domain. A checklist panel simulates the output of expertise without the underlying causal model. In 'edge cases' (the Expert's Blindspot vs Panel's Ceiling, SCR_006), the expert's intuition detects anomalies that the checklist ignores, whereas the panel will confidently check all boxes and arrive at a wrong answer.","optional_refs":["c05c3de669a2f3d14fde3a7087963463da852b1397db4532a7cb70ccd31ddc3b"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["c05c3de669a2f3d14fde3a7087963463da852b1397db4532a7cb70ccd31ddc3b"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Genuine reliability gain is distinguished from mere compliance by the presence of 'cognitive friction'—documented instances where the panel struggled with or questioned a checklist item before arriving at the correct answer.","counterconditions":["The panel is incentivized for speed, masking cognitive friction.","The checklist is designed so simply that no friction is required for correct outcomes."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Mere compliance is characterized by a uniform distribution of effort across checklist items (SCR_001). Genuine reliability is evidenced by a non-uniform distribution of time and justification (SCR_007), where 'time-spikes' correlate with the objective difficulty of the item. The evidence is the 'divergent internal audit trail' (SRC_002) showing the panel actively wrestled with the data rather than blindly ticking boxes.","optional_refs":["333eb92f27922e603ca0596692d9e0e1041290b0971b44e2d3060e4a3ce55f3a","ba195d863b44e05fae4e064eb581457ba6a5501527a698d6ca85c6dc2ebe731a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["333eb92f27922e603ca0596692d9e0e1041290b0971b44e2d3060e4a3ce55f3a","ba195d863b44e05fae4e064eb581457ba6a5501527a698d6ca85c6dc2ebe731a"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The panel is more reliable only when the 'error-type symmetry' shifts the risk from catastrophic omission to manageable commission.","counterconditions":["The 'commission errors' of the panel are more catastrophic than the 'omission errors' of the expert.","The verification process is as prone to error as the expert's intuition."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Expert failure is often 'silent' (forgetting a critical check), while panel failure is 'noisy' (misinterpreting a check). In tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, 'noisy' errors are more easily caught by a second layer of review or the verification process itself. The reliability gain is not in the binary correctness of a single run, but in the lower variance of outcomes across multiple cases (SCR_003).","optional_refs":["23c3c1ab286de1ab33f8d0403592cdbd9d18ccb554598e340edf5d7af8ea5dec"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["23c3c1ab286de1ab33f8d0403592cdbd9d18ccb554598e340edf5d7af8ea5dec"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Reliability is a U-shaped curve based on training depth; superficial training creates 'pseudo-experts' who are less reliable than both the true expert and a totally untrained person following a checklist.","counterconditions":["Training is either nonexistent or exhaustive (reaching expert-level understanding).","The checklist is explicitly framed as a 'minimum viable' tool rather than an exhaustive guide."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Moderate training leads to 'overconfidence in the tool' (SCR_004), where the panel believes the checklist is an exhaustive map of the territory. This eliminates the healthy skepticism that a true expert possesses and the blind obedience of a total novice, creating a 'danger zone' of confidence without competence.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The distinguishing evidence of reliability gain is the panel's ability to correctly reject 'decoy' items within the checklist through causal justification.","counterconditions":["The panel is told that all boxes must be checked regardless of relevance.","The decoy items are indistinguishable from critical items without expert-level domain knowledge."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"If a checklist contains 'decoy' prompts (items that look relevant but are not for a specific case), a compliance-driven panel will check them anyway to 'finish the list.' A reliability-driven panel will reject the decoy and provide a justification based on the case's specific facts (SCR_007). The presence of justified rejections proves the panel is reasoning, not just complying.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6}],"context_request":{"desired_retrieval_channels":[],"requested_refs":["f9d456a6572a171c270f70cec40c3dfafd6472b14300a5ca2ea4e17d70bfd358","ba195d863b44e05fae4e064eb581457ba6a5501527a698d6ca85c6dc2ebe731a","c05c3de669a2f3d14fde3a7087963463da852b1397db4532a7cb70ccd31ddc3b","333eb92f27922e603ca0596692d9e0e1041290b0971b44e2d3060e4a3ce55f3a","23c3c1ab286de1ab33f8d0403592cdbd9d18ccb554598e340edf5d7af8ea5dec"]},"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Contradiction: If the panel is 'trained non-experts', at what point does the training make them 'experts'? If the training is sufficient to allow them to justify rejecting decoys (SCR_007), they have effectively acquired the domain expertise the problem seeks to replace. The 'panel' advantage may actually be a hidden 'distributed expertise' advantage, not a 'checklist' advantage.","why_keep_this":"Challenges the premise of the 'non-expert' label."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[]},"simulation_proposals":[]}